{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>Because on account of this thing HaShem will bless you.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory (Shabbat 151a) learned from the expression \"בִּגְלַל\" that it is a wheel that turns in the world. And many have wondered at this expression, for \"בִּגְלַל\" is not the language of \"גַּלְגַּל\" (wheel), since one גִּימֶ\"ל is missing. And I say that their intention of blessed memory was to explain \"בִּגְלַל\" from the expression \"וְגָלְלוּ אֶת הָאֶבֶן\" (Genesis 29:3), meaning: when poverty rolls among the people from this one to that one in its accustomed manner, then HaShem will bless you and cause that it not roll and not fall upon you in its falling upon people. And it follows of itself that we hear that the matter turns in the world like a wheel, for every giver ends up being a receiver, for such is the measure in all existing things throughout the three worlds, that every giver is destined to be a receiver, in the manner that we explained above in parashat בְּרֵאשִׁית on the verse \"יוֹם הַשִּׁשִּׁי\" — that in all existing things the matter proceeds by causes like a wheel, that each one of them gives and receives; except HaShem alone, who gives and does not receive." "And the reason for this matter appears to be: that HaShem did not desire that man establish himself in the land — he and his seed — forever, along the lines of what is stated (Leviticus 25:23): \"וְהָאָרֶץ לֹא תִמָּכֵר לִצְמִתֻת כִּי לִי הָאָרֶץ\". And were man never to come to this measure, neither he nor his seed, he would hold the land in perpetuity and forget that to HaShem belongs the land. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, brings it about that every man comes to this measure, either he or his seed, to know and to make known that all are like sojourners on the earth, and to HaShem belong the earth and its fullness. And this is what it says after the verse \"כִּי בִּגְלַל\", to give a reason why it rolls among people: \"כִּי לֹא יֶחְדַּל אֶבְיוֹן מִקֶּרֶב הָאָרֶץ\", for \"קֶרֶב הָאָרֶץ\" requires this matter as stated. \"עַל כֵּן אָנֹכִי מְצַוְּךָ לֵאמֹר פָּתֹחַ תִּפְתַּח אֶת יָדְךָ לַעֲנִיֶּיךָ\", so that they will give also to you or to your seed, but not to both, as is written (Psalms 37:25): \"וְלֹא רָאִיתִי צַדִּיק נֶעֱזָב וְזַרְעוֹ מְבַקֶּשׁ לָחֶם\", meaning: he and his seed — rather, either he or his seed. And by this this verse does not contradict what our Rabbis of blessed memory say (Shabbat 151a): either he or his seed come to this measure. And some say: the missing גִּימֶ\"ל in the word \"בִּגְלַל\" comes to say that this results from the sin of withholding loving-kindness, for גִּימֶ\"ל דָּלֶ\"ת — our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Shabbat 104a): גְּמוֹל דַּלִּים (reward the poor). And the missing of the גִּימֶ\"ל toward the poor brings about the wheel."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:12:34.597894390Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}